  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio NextGen\n\nContext that shapes every finding below: this is a **fully static marketing site** (Next.js 16.2.10 / React 19.2.4, App Router, no database, no API routes, no client-side data fetching). So classic \"N+1 query\" and \"cache the DB call\" issues don't literally apply — the real equivalents are repeated in-memory lookups and a pervasive Server/Client Component boundary problem that costs bundle size and hydration time on *every* page. That last one is the highest-leverage fix here.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Biggest issue: nearly the whole app is unnecessarily Client Components\n\n`grep \"use client\"` turns up **26 of ~30 component files** — including full content pages like `ArticlePage.tsx`, `ProductPage.tsx`, `LegalPage.tsx`, `InsightsIndex.tsx`, `ProductsIndex.tsx`, `AboutPage.tsx`, `ContactPage.tsx`.\n\nPer Next's own docs (`node_modules/next/dist/docs/.../05-server-and-client-components.md`): *\"Once a file is marked with `'use client'`, all of its imports and the components it directly renders are included in the client bundle.\"* Because `ArticlePage` is `\"use client\"`, its import `RichText.tsx` (a pure, hook-free renderer for ~1000+ words of prose) gets pulled into the client bundle too, even though it has zero interactivity — same for `ProductBlocks.tsx`.\n\nConcretely: every article/product/legal page ships `motion/react` + the full page component + the entire article body as client JS and hydrates it, when the actual interactive surface is tiny (a TOC anchor list, a category filter, a hover preview).\n\n**Fix** — keep pages as Server Components, isolate interactivity into small client islands:\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/pages/ArticlePage.tsx — make this a Server Component\nimport RichText from \"@/components/site/RichText\";      // stays server-rendered\nimport ArticleMotionShell from \"./ArticleMotionShell\";  // \"use client\" — just the reveal wrappers\n\nexport default function ArticlePage({ article, body }: { article: Article; body: RichBlock[] }) {\n  const toc = buildToc(body);\n  return (\n    <SiteShell>\n      <ArticleMotionShell article={article}>\n        <RichText blocks={body} />   {/* server-rendered, no client JS for the prose */}\n      </ArticleMotionShell>\n      {toc.length > 1 && <TocNav toc={toc} />}\n    </SiteShell>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n`InsightsIndex.tsx` and `ProductsIndex.tsx` should similarly stay server components with just `ArticleCard`'s filter buttons / `ProductRow`'s hover state as client leaves. This also unlocks the new **Cache Components** primitives this Next version ships (`use cache`, `cacheLife`, `cacheTag` — confirmed present in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/01-directives/use-cache.md`), which currently can't apply to any of this content because it's all client-rendered.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders\n\n**`Preloader.tsx:37-44`** — the load counter calls `setProgress()` on *every* `requestAnimationFrame` tick for 1.5s (~90 renders), re-rendering the whole preloader tree each time just to update a 3-digit number:\n\n```tsx\nconst tick = (now: number) => {\n  const t = Math.min((now - start) / LOAD_MS, 1);\n  const eased = t === 1 ? 1 : 1 - Math.pow(2, -10 * t);\n  setProgress(Math.round(eased * 100));   // fires ~90x over 1.5s\n  ...\n};\n```\nFix: skip the `setState` when the rounded value hasn't changed, or write the digits directly to a ref'd DOM node and bypass React entirely for this counter (it's purely cosmetic text, not something other components read).\n\n```tsx\nconst progressRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\nconst tick = (now: number) => {\n  const t = Math.min((now - start) / LOAD_MS, 1);\n  const eased = t === 1 ? 1 : 1 - Math.pow(2, -10 * t);\n  if (progressRef.current) {\n    progressRef.current.textContent = Math.round(eased * 100).toString().padStart(3, \"0\");\n  }\n  ...\n};\n```\n\n**`InsightsIndex.tsx:70-73`** and **`ArticlePage.tsx:28-30`** — filters run inline on every render instead of being memoized:\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/pages/InsightsIndex.tsx\nconst filtered =\n  active === \"All\" ? ARTICLES_SORTED : ARTICLES_SORTED.filter((a) => a.category === active);\n```\nCheap today at 9 articles, but it recreates a new array reference every render (defeating any future `memo()` on `ArticleCard`) and won't scale. Wrap in `useMemo(() => ..., [active])`. Same for `ArticlePage.tsx:28`'s `related` computation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. N+1-shaped patterns (closest analogue in a DB-less app)\n\n**`src/lib/articles.ts:171-173`** and **`src/lib/products.tsx:521`** do an `O(n)` `Array.find()` scan, and it's called **twice per route** — once in `generateMetadata`, once in the page component (`src/app/insights/[slug]/page.tsx:18,32`, `src/app/products/[slug]/page.tsx:18,32`):\n\n```ts\nexport function getArticle(slug: string): Article | undefined {\n  return ARTICLES.find((a) => a.slug === slug);\n}\n```\n\nAt n≈9–14 and `dynamicParams = false` (build-time SSG only), the runtime cost to actual visitors is zero — but it's the exact pattern that becomes a real N+1 the moment this content moves to a CMS or DB. Fix now while it's cheap:\n\n```ts\nconst ARTICLES_BY_SLUG = new Map(ARTICLES.map((a) => [a.slug, a]));\nexport function getArticle(slug: string): Article | undefined {\n  return ARTICLES_BY_SLUG.get(slug);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\nGood news: the client-side effects in this codebase are disciplined. `SmoothScroll.tsx` cancels its `requestAnimationFrame` loop and calls `lenis.destroy()` on unmount; `Nav.tsx` and `MarketConstellation.tsx`'s `SceneRig` remove their `scroll`/`pointermove` listeners; `Terrain` explicitly disposes its manually-created `THREE.BufferGeometry` (`MarketConstellation.tsx:58`) — R3F auto-disposes the JSX-declared materials/geometries elsewhere in that file. **No unmounted-listener or detached-node leaks found.**\n\nOne thing worth a second look, though not a leak: `Nav.tsx:31` and `Preloader.tsx:33,51` both write directly to `document.documentElement.style.overflow` from independent effects with no shared owner:\n\n```tsx\n// Nav.tsx\ndocument.documentElement.style.overflow = open ? \"hidden\" : \"\";\n```\n```tsx\n// Preloader.tsx\ndocument.documentElement.style.overflow = \"hidden\";\n```\nIf a mobile-nav interaction and a preloader lock were ever active at once, one effect's cleanup (`= \"\"`) can clobber the other's lock. Not a leak, but worth centralizing into one scroll-lock hook with a ref-counted lock if you add more places that need this.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n**`MarketConstellation.tsx:60-69`** — the real hot path in this codebase. `Terrain`'s `useFrame` recomputes `waveY()` (3 `sin`/`cos` pairs) for all `COLS * ROWS = 6,050` points, every animation frame:\n\n```tsx\nuseFrame((state) => {\n  if (reduced) return;\n  const t = state.clock.getElapsedTime();\n  for (let i = 0; i < baseX.length; i++) {\n    arr[i * 3 + 1] = waveY(baseX[i], baseZ[i], t);   // ~6,050 × 6 transcendental calls, 60x/sec\n  }\n  attr.needsUpdate = true;\n});\n```\nThat's on the order of 360k+ `sin`/`cos` evaluations per second on the JS main thread for a background decoration. Three concrete options, cheapest first:\n- **Throttle**: update every 2nd frame (`if (frameCount++ % 2) return`) — halves the cost, motion is described as \"calm, breathing\" so 30fps displacement is visually indistinguishable.\n- **Scale resolution to viewport**: drop `COLS`/`ROWS` on narrow/mobile viewports where the grid is barely visible anyway.\n- **Move to GPU**: push `waveY` into a vertex shader (`onBeforeCompile` or a custom `ShaderMaterial`) and drive it off a `uTime` uniform — this is the standard fix for exactly this \"displaced points terrain\" pattern and removes the main-thread cost entirely.\n\n**`Trust.tsx:7-23`** and **`AboutPage.tsx:82-97`** — `ticks()` / `CompassSeal`'s tick generator re-runs `Array.from` + trig on every render even though its inputs (`count, r1, r2`) never change:\n\n```tsx\nfunction ticks(count: number, r1: number, r2: number, strokeWidth: number) {\n  return Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => { /* cos/sin */ });\n}\n```\nLow impact since these sections have no state and only render on mount, but since it's pure output of constants, hoist the two call sites (`ticks(28, ...)`, `ticks(4, ...)`) to module scope as precomputed constants — zero downside.\n\n---\n\n### Priority order\n1. **Server/Client Component split** (§1) — the one change that reduces shipped JS and hydration cost on every page of the site.\n2. **`MarketConstellation` per-frame cost** (§5) — throttle or GPU-shift; only real CPU hot path found.\n3. **Preloader per-frame `setState`** (§2) — trivial fix, removes ~90 redundant renders per page load.\n4. Map-based lookups + `useMemo` on filters (§2, §3) — cheap now, prevents scaling pain later.\n",
